{"tool_name":"bayespeak","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"bayespeak","home":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/BayesPeak.html","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"This package is an implementation of the BayesPeak algorithm for peak-calling in ChIP-seq data.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:59:51Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-30T16:15:33Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.26.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:bayespeak","credit":[{"email":"jonathan.cairns@babraham.ac.uk","name":"Jonathan Cairns","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/BayesPeak.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Peak calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3222"}]}],"name":"BayesPeak","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/biostatistics/kxr029","metadata":{"abstract":"Chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by next generation sequencing (ChIP-seq) is a powerful technique that is being used in a wide range of biological studies including genome-wide measurements of protein-DNA interactions, DNA methylation, and histone modifications. The vast amount of data and biases introduced by sequencing and/or genome mapping pose new challenges and call for effective methods and fast computer programs for statistical analysis. To systematically model ChIP-seq data, we build a dynamic signal profile for each chromosome and then model the profile using a fully Bayesian hidden Ising model. The proposed model naturally takes into account spatial dependency and global and local distributions of sequence tags. It can be used for one-sample and two-sample analyses. Through model diagnosis, the proposed method can detect falsely enriched regions caused by sequencing and/or mapping errors, which is usually not offered by the existing hypothesis-testing-based methods. The proposed method is illustrated using 3 transcription factor (TF) ChIP-seq data sets and 2 mixed ChIP-seq data sets and compared with 4 popular and/or well-documented methods: MACS, CisGenome, BayesPeak, and SISSRs. The results indicate that the proposed method achieves equivalent or higher sensitivity and spatial resolution in detecting TF binding sites with false discovery rate at a much lower level. \u00a9 The Author 2011.","authors":[{"name":"Mo Q."}],"citationCount":14,"date":"2012-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Biostatistics","title":"A fully Bayesian hidden Ising model for ChIP-seq data analysis"},"pmid":"21914728","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"ChIP-seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3169"},{"term":"DNA binding sites","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3125"},{"term":"Nucleic acid sites, features and motifs","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3511"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"BayesPeak","home":"https://bio.tools/bayespeak","license":"GPL-2.0","version":"1.26.0","summary":"This package is an implementation of the BayesPeak algorithm for peak-calling in ChIP-seq data.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:bayespeak:1.26.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":7,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2012"},{"count":1,"year":"2014"},{"count":1,"year":"2015"},{"count":4,"year":"2013"}],"doi":"10.1093/biostatistics/kxr029","pmid":"21914728","ref_count":0,"refs":[],"title":"A fully Bayesian hidden Ising model for ChIP-seq data analysis.","year":2011}]}],"summary":{"description":true